@inproceedings{Balaji2018,
  title={When is graph reordering an optimization? studying the effect of lightweight graph reordering across applications and input graphs},
  author={Balaji, Vignesh and Lucia, Brandon},
  booktitle={2018 IEEE International Symposium on Workload Characterization (IISWC)},
  pages={203--214},
  year={2018},
  organization={IEEE},
  comments={
    OBSERVATIONS: Has a diagram of graph - to - memory. ID five bottleneck exploitation techniques 
      (1) Graph Reordering {rewrite graph in memory so that when read in order it works better} [Gorder, Recall, LLP, Nested Dissection, Slashburn, RCM, Rabbit], 
      (2) Cache Blocking {break graph into subgraphs that reduce irregularity of data accesses} [Graphicionado, Graphmat, Beamer2017, ] 
      (3) Vertex Scheduling {Optimize the order vertices are visited} [Naiad] 
      (4) Graph Partitioning {Reduce inter-node communications by creating graph partitions with minimal links between partitions - mostly for distributed computing} [Giraph, Powergraph, Gps, Pregel]
      (5) In-memory Graph Processing {compress the graphs so that they fit in memory of a single machine} [Ligra, GraphMAT, Ligra+, GraphPad, GraphLab,]
    PROBLEMS: The primary source of inefficiency in single-node graph processing is the poor locality in the processor cache hierarchy. Graph processing applications exhibit an irregular memory access pattern which leads to poor spatial and temporal locality in the memory access stream. Consequently, graph applications make sub-optimal use of the cache hierarchy and incur many long-latency main memory accesses - with upto 80 percent of execution time stalled on main memory accesses.
    SOLUTIONS: The locality of graph applications can be improved with better graph data layout. Graph reordering techniques relabel vertices with different IDs by exploiting the structural properties of graphs to improve locality. We ran Gorder , a state of the art reordering algorithm, and observed that it took around 15 hours to reorder a graph which reduced the run time of the Page Rank graph application from 12 seconds to 5 seconds. lightweight graph reordering techniques are required that can provide a net performance improvement even after including the overhead of reordering.
  }
}

