@inproceedings{Dadu2021,
  title={Polygraph: Exposing the value of flexibility for graph processing accelerators},
  author={Dadu, Vidushi and Liu, Sihao and Nowatzki, Tony},
  booktitle={2021 ACM/IEEE 48th Annual International Symposium on Computer Architecture (ISCA)},
  pages={595--608},
  year={2021},
  organization={IEEE},
  abstract={Because of the importance of graph workloads and the limitations of CPUs/GPUs, many graph processing accelerators have been proposed. The basic approach of prior accelerators is to focus on a single graph algorithm variant (eg. bulk-synchronous + slicing). While helpful for specialization, this leaves performance potential from flexibility on the table and also complicates understanding the relationship between graph types, workloads, algorithms, and specialization.In this work, we explore the value of flexibility in graph processing accelerators. First, we identify a taxonomy of key algorithm variants. Then we develop a template architecture (PolyGraph) that is flexible across these variants while being able to modularly integrate specialization features for each.Overall we find that flexibility in graph acceleration is critical. If only one variant can be supported, asynchronous-updates/priority-vertex-scheduling/graph-slicing is the best design, achieving 1.93x speedup over the best-performing accelerator, GraphPulse. However, static flexibility per-workload can further improve performance by 2.71x. With dynamic flexibility per-phase, performance further improves by up to 50%.},
  comment={
    OBSERVATIONS: 
    PiUMA Competitor
    (1) Graph worloads offer specialization opportunities: commutative updates, a resilience to performing work in an arbitrary order, and repetitive structure in memory access and computation. This implies large advantages for specialization, either with CPU offload engines or accelerators .
    (2) There are four graph algorithm variants (See Figure 1 in paper for spectrum): 
      (a) Update Visibility: defines when writes become visible to other computations, and hence this affects the granularity at which new tasks are created. Writes may become visible after one pass through the graph (graph-synchronous), after each slice (slice-synchronous), or immediately (asynchronous). Graph and Slice and both bulk-synchronus. 
      (b) Vertex Scheduling: defines the processing order for active vertices, relevant for asynchronous variants. Include: 
        (i) Locality order: To improve the vertex access locality, schedule by vertex-id. 
        (ii) Creation order: Schedule vertices in the order they are activated. 
        (iii) Work-efficiency order: Schedule vertices in an order which reduces redundant work.
      (c) Slice Scheduling: defines whether the working set may be limited to a predefined slice of all graph vertices. Slices are determined during offline partitioning and are generally sized to fit on-chip memory. Updates to data outside the current slice are deferred, and an explicit phase is required to switch slices. Slices can be scheduled in different orders, forming new variants.Types:
        (i) Round-robin: iterate through all slices. 
        (ii) Locality: similar, but repeatedly process each slice. 
        (iii) Work-efficiency: prioritize slices whose properties change most
      (d) Update Direction: efines whether a task updates its own property (pull/remote read), or whether a task updates its neighbor's properties (push/remote atomic update).

    (3) Performance is a product of work-efficiency and throughput

    PROBLEMS: 
    (1) Graph processing workloads are challenging for traditional architectures (CPUs/GPUs) due to data-dependent memory access, reuse, and parallelism.
    (2) Hardware accelerators typically have to make assumptions about the input graph type, workload property and fundamental graph algorithm variants, all of which compine to affect the tradeoff between throughput and work efficency. 
    (3) The fundamental limitation of existing decoupled-spatial accelerators is the lack of support for fine-grain data-dependent parallelism (ie. task parallelism)
    (4) Most algortihms are iterative and convergant. For many algorithms like a BFS, or a GCN this means that they are order-insensitive. However, for some graph algorithms list Shortest Path, their work-efficiency is order-sensitive. 
    (5) Dense-frontier workloads like PageRank and Collaborative Filtering usually have more than 50\% active vertices, while sparse frontier workloads like SSSP and BFS (traversal-based) have fewer. Sparser frontier workloads require fewer passes through the graph until convergance. 

    SOLUTIONS:
    (1) Our insight is that we can optimize performance by having the flexibility to use the right algorithm variant for the right graph and workload (or even workload phase)
    (2) Our design augments prior efficient decoupled-spatial accelerators which can support general data-structures and suit both memory-intensive (eg. Breadth-first Search (BFS)) and compute-intensive workloads (eg. Graph Convolutional Networks (GCN)).
    METHODS:
    (1) Barriers in synchronus algorithms can be an overhead when the work between them is low. 
    (2) Storage-order access increases spatial locality at the cost of work-efficency. Creation order is simple FIFO logic and work-efficency order requires dynamic sorting. 
    (3) non-Slicing avoids barriers and slice-switching data movement. Slicing can hard optimal ordering by limiting the scheduling scope. Slicing does make more effective use of on-chip memory.
    (4) Push reduces communication bandwidth and better hides communication latency. Pull-based methods with tuned pre-fetching that are based on vertex convergance can be more effective.
    PRIMITIVES:
  }
}

@article{Lenharth2016,
  title={Parallel graph analytics},
  author={Lenharth, Andrew and Nguyen, Donald and Pingali, Keshav},
  journal={Communications of the ACM},
  volume={59},
  number={5},
  pages={78--87},
  year={2016},
  publisher={ACM New York, NY, USA},
  comments={ . There are three main challenges in performing computations on these graphs—large graph sizes, diversity in graph structure, and the complex patterns of parallelism in graph analytics applications
    OBSERVATIONS:
     (1) Many graph analytics problems can be solved by both topology-driven and data-driven algorithms, but each type of algorithm makes different trade-offs.
     (2) Topology-driven algorithms are easier to implement because graph representations usually store the nodes of the graph in an array or a list, so iteration over active nodes can be implemented simply as iteration over this data structure
     (3) data-driven algorithms must use a workset (or multiset) to hold the active nodes. Sequential implementations usually use lists and priority queues to imple- ment worksets for unordered and ordered algorithms, respectively. 
     (4) Data-driven algorithms can be more work efficient. Data-driven algorithms can focus on regions of the graph where useful work needs to be done, making them asymptotically faster than their topology-driven coun- terparts for many problems


    GRAPH ALGORITHM TAXONOMY:
    The `operator formulation of algorithms' is a data centric abstraction of algortihms:
      (1) Local View: Described by an operator.
        (a) Operators: A graph update rule applied to an active node in the graph. Each operator application is called an activity and reads and writes the neighborhood of the graph around the active node. 
          (i) Morph: Modify the structure of the neighborhood by adding and removing nodes and edges. 
          (ii) Local Computation:  update only lavels on nodes and edges without changing the structure. 
            (01) Pull style: reads neighboring nodes and writes to active node. 
            (02)Push style: Reads active node and writes to neighboring nodes. 
          (iii) Reader: Reads data that is read-only
      (2) Global View: Describes the locations of active nodes in the graph and the order which they must appear
        (a) Types of Algorithms: 
          (i) Topology-Driven: the locations of active nodes are determined by the graph structure. These algorithms make a number of sweeps over the graph; in each such sweep, all nodes are initially active, and the sweeps terminate when some global quiescence condition is reached. (e.g. Bellman-Ford)
          (ii) Data-Driven: There is an initial set of active nodes, and the execution of activities may cause other nodes to become active on the fly. These algorithms do not have any algorithmic notion of sweeps, terminating when there are no more active nodes in the graph (e.g. Djirkstra's)
        (b) Ordering:
          (i) Unordered: any order of processing active nodes is semantically correct. Some orders may be more efficient than others, so unordered algorithms sometimes assign soft priorities to activities
          (ii) Ordered: requiring active nodes appear to have been processed in a specific order, specified by assigning priorities to active nodes, and the implementation is required to process active nodes so they appear to have been scheduled for execution in strict priority order


    PROBLEMS:
    (1) Large graph sizes. In todays era of big data, parallel graph analytics systems must deal with graphs with billions of nodes and trillions of edges. Parallel computing is essential for analyzing properties of graphs of such size in reasonable time. 
    (2) Diversity in graph structure means that for many analytics problems, different algorithms must be used for uniform-degree, random, and power-law graphs to obtain good parallel performance:
      (a) Planar graphs and graphs (such as road networks) have a uniform degree distribution that does not depend on the size of the graph the “average diameter of the graph, increases rapidly with graph size.
      (b) Social network graphs have a diameter that grows as the logarithm of the number of nodes. The degree distributions follow a power-law structure.
      (c) Random Graphs start with some number of nodes and then add edges incrementally between ran- domly selected pairs of nodes.
    (3) Need for new parallel abstractions. Most existing abstractions and implementations for parallel computing were developed for data parallelism. Some graph analytics algorithms have data parallelism, but others exhibit a more complex parallelism pattern called “amorphous” data-parallelism. Tasks in amorphous data-parallel programs may or may not be able to run in parallel, depending on the graph structure and values known only at runtime.To exploit this parallelism pattern, systems need to find opportunities for parallel execution while executing the program in parallel. As a consequence, abstractions and implementations for data-parallelism are not adequate for many parallel graph analytics algorithms.
    (4) Amorphous Data-Parallelism: Since neighborhoods can overlap, the memory model, which defines the semantics of reads and writes in overlapped regions, may prevent some activities with overlapping neighborhoods from executing in parallel. In addition, ordering constraints between activities must be enforced.
    SOLUTIONS:
    METHODS:
    PRIMITIVES:
  }
}

